          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exploring Some Geometric Themes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.088,
            0.846,
            0.127
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "? Find another object that makes the same projection as that of a given cone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.139,
            0.847,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For this reason, we often take three mutually perpendicular projections of an object. As shown in the figure, we consider a plane in front of the object called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "vertical plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", a plane below the object called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "horizontal plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and a plane to the side of the object called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "side plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.24,
            0.844,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.213,
                0.24,
                0.844,
                0.662
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_21_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.677,
            0.847,
            0.772
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We give names to the projections on these three planes based on the directions from which the object is viewed. The projection on the vertical plane is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "front view",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", on the horizontal plane the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "top view",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and on the side plane the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "side view",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". These projections formalise the notion of profiles that we explored in an earlier section."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.78,
            0.845,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Let us see what these projections are for the objects shown in Fig. 4.6, when the planes shown are taken to be vertical planes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.822,
            0.591,
            0.844
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Projections of the different lines in Fig. 4.6 —"
